  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14222967/assets/be5f6207531c18acecdb6a7ba3890e23913de4a78546db620f2986dd0467fa6d.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-0.5)+f(0)+f(0.5)+f(1)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(2+3+4+5)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =14\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[-1,1],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area.",
